{
  "title": "Drive the recipe sync race tests' in-flight writes through the API",
  "description": "The tests in `tests/test_recipe_sync_races.py` hold their \
uncommitted change open in a direct database transaction that mirrors the \
server's write statements. They were written that way because a stalled API \
write used to wedge the whole server (the old \
`p2-blocking-db-calls-stall-the-server`): the sync under test would never have \
been answered.\n\
\n\
Database work now runs on the blocking thread pool, and \
`tests/test_server_liveness.py` proves the server keeps answering while a \
write waits on a row lock. So the race tests could instead drive the in-flight \
write through the real API -- start the write in a background thread, block it \
on a row lock held by a helper transaction, run the sync, then release the \
lock -- and thereby also cover the handlers' `change_xid` stamping inside the \
race window instead of trusting the mirrored SQL to stay in sync with the \
handlers. (The sequential-delta coverage of stamping in `test_recipe_sync.py` \
stays; this adds the racing variant.)\n\
\n\
Worth doing only if it keeps the tests deterministic: the lock-release \
choreography must not introduce flakiness. If the API-driven variant cannot be \
made reliably deterministic, keep the direct-transaction approach and close \
this issue.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 3,
